KickServ @VERSION@ (@DATE@)
===========================

KickServ is an AmigaOS 4 commodity without a window. It serves the
Kickstart modules in SYS:Kickstart over TCP/IP, so that KickSync, a
program on another computer, can update the Kickstart.zip that AmigaOS 4
under QEMU (with BBoot) boots from:

  KickSync (Linux, macOS, Windows)        KickServ (AmigaOS 4)
    reads Kickstart.zip         --TCP-->    serves SYS:Kickstart
    asks for the version of every module    reports size, date, $VER
    downloads the newer ones                sends the file
    writes Kickstart_<date>.zip

Source, KickSync and the protocol: https://github.com/orgin/Kickstart-Sync


Installation
------------

Double-click Install. It copies KickServ and its icon to
SYS:Utilities/Commodities and this text to SYS:Documentation, and asks
whether KickServ should start when the computer starts (it is then added
to the WBStartup preferences, Prefs/WBStartup) and whether to start it now.

An existing KickServ icon is kept, with its tool types.


Starting and stopping
---------------------

Double-click KickServ, or start it from a Shell. It appears in Exchange:

  Disable   stops listening (the TCP port is freed)
  Enable    listens again
  Remove    quits KickServ

Ctrl-C quits it too when it was started from a Shell. Starting it a second
time does nothing.


Tool types and Shell arguments
------------------------------

  PORT=<n>          TCP port to listen on                 (default 6800)
  DIR=<path>        drawer whose files are served      (default SYS:Kickstart)
  ALLOW=<list>      comma separated addresses or networks that may
                    connect, e.g. 10.0.2.2 or 192.168.1.0/24; ANY allows
                    everybody                                (default ANY)
  CX_PRIORITY=<n>   commodity priority                       (default 0)
  QUIET             no log output when started from a Shell

The icon has them switched off with brackets, e.g. (PORT=6800): remove
the brackets to use one. From a Shell the same keywords work as arguments
(KickServ PORT=7000) and override the icon.

Only plain file names inside DIR are served: names with ":", a leading
"/" or "//" are refused, so nothing outside that drawer can be read.
KickServ has no password, so use ALLOW if the computer can be reached
from a network you don't trust.


Using it with QEMU
------------------

With QEMU's user networking, forward a port of the host computer to the
guest, for example:

  qemu-system-ppc ... -nic user,model=rtl8139,hostfwd=tcp:127.0.0.1:6800-:6800

(or add hostfwd=tcp:127.0.0.1:6800-:6800 to an existing -netdev user,...).
Connections then come from 10.0.2.2 inside the guest, so ALLOW=10.0.2.2
lets only the host in. On the host:

  KickSync localhost:6800 Kickstart.zip

shows what is newer on the Amiga and writes Kickstart_<YYYY-MM-DD>.zip;
the original zip is never changed. KickSync -n only shows the list, and
KickSync --list localhost:6800 lists what KickServ offers.

KickSync never replaces Kicklayout, which belongs to one particular
machine: when the Amiga's copy differs, it lists the MODULE lines found in
only one of the two copies, so you can merge them by hand.


